1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Washington, D.C. is the home of the United States Naval Research Laboratory.

2
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:36,600
The laboratory was founded in 1923 upon the recommendation of a special naval board of consultants headed by Thomas A. Edison.

3
00:00:37,399 --> 00:00:46,679
Here, some 3,000 scientists, engineers, and supporting personnel are engaged in applied and basic research in the physical sciences.

4
00:00:46,679 --> 00:00:57,399
In the field of radio astronomy, this 50-foot radio telescope has been used to measure the temperature of the planets Mars and Venus.

5
00:00:59,939 --> 00:01:06,480
Intensive work has been carried out in upper atmosphere research, culminating in the launching of Earth's satellites.

6
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:20,620
In the field of nuclear physics, a variety of instruments is used, including a 6-million-volt

7
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:30,320
proton Van de Graaff, a 20-million-electron-volt Betatron, and two Cockcroft-Walton accelerators.

8
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:41,180
Right in 1952, laboratory scientists planned another tool, a nuclear research reactor.

9
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:53,380
They decided on a pool type reactor, modified to incorporate a thermal column and beam ports.

10
00:01:53,379 --> 00:02:01,399
The design consists of a pool, a movable bridge from which the core is suspended at a depth

11
00:02:01,399 --> 00:02:10,819
of 16 feet out in the pool or in a graphite-walled niche where the shielding is penetrated by

12
00:02:10,819 --> 00:02:14,340
beam ports.

13
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:23,180
Through these ports, beams of radiation stream to the experimental area outside the shield.

14
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:28,520
A thermal column provides the source of thermal neutrons.

15
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:34,819
Pneumatic rabbit systems are used to irradiate samples for short times.

16
00:02:34,819 --> 00:02:38,060
The reactor consists of a core,

17
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:43,379
detectors, and control rods.

18
00:02:43,379 --> 00:02:49,520
In the pool, the 150,000 gallons of water surrounding the core serves as an

19
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,300
effective radiation shield.

20
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:57,500
additional concrete shielding eight feet thick is provided around the niche.

21
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:06,140
Construction began in 1955 in one end of an existing warehouse.

22
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:11,580
With the exception of the building modifications and the concrete construction,

23
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:17,100
all design and fabrication were performed by laboratory personnel.

24
00:03:17,099 --> 00:03:24,460
Fuel elements were obtained from the Oak Ridge National Laboratory.

25
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:32,299
On September 17, 1956, the reactor was brought to criticality.

26
00:03:32,299 --> 00:03:37,500
Operation is presently limited by license agreement with the Atomic Energy Commission

27
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:41,900
to 100 kilowatts.

28
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:48,360
Before entering the reactor area, all visitors are required to register and to receive radiation

29
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:51,099
badges.

30
00:03:51,099 --> 00:03:59,060
All possible measures are taken to protect the health and safety of personnel.

31
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:09,700
The reactor pool is 40 feet long, 26 feet and 20 feet deep.

32
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,140
The instrument bridge is in the foreground.

33
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:23,240
And beyond it is the reactor bridge, from which the reactor core is suspended.

34
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:27,500
A fuel element is placed on the loading elevator.

35
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:34,360
Lead shields at the top of the element reduce the gamma radiation in the vertical direction.

36
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:41,560
Each element consists of two aluminum side pieces containing 18 thin uranium-aluminum

37
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:44,259
plates.

38
00:04:44,259 --> 00:04:52,460
The fuel portion of each plate is a sheet of uranium-aluminum alloy, 20 mils thick.

39
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:58,220
The fuel is 90% enriched uranium-235.

40
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:03,120
Twenty mil aluminum cladding protects the fuel from corrosion and contains the fission

41
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:09,780
fragments. Water rising by natural convection between the fuel plates

42
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:15,500
removes the fission heat. Natural convection cooling is adequate to

43
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:23,519
several hundred kilowatts. At higher power, forceful coolant will be used. A

44
00:05:23,519 --> 00:05:32,840
complete fuel element contains about 140 grams of uranium-235. Loading of the

45
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:39,160
core begins by lowering new elements into the pool. The core is assembled by

46
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:44,420
coupling on to the fuel elements with a long handling tool and transferring

47
00:05:44,420 --> 00:05:49,940
these elements underwater to a grid positioning plate. In the pool where

48
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:56,260
water is the sole reflecting medium it takes about 3,400 grams of uranium to

49
00:05:56,260 --> 00:06:02,540
form a critical mass. In the niche where three sides of the core are reflected by

50
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:10,920
graphite, only 2,700 grams are required. The individual elements are positioned

51
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:14,379
into a rectangular array.

52
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:27,980
The final element completes a core configuration in which the uranium mass

53
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:32,980
slightly exceeds that required for criticality.

54
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:45,980
The position and history of every element is recorded.

55
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:51,980
The geometry of the core can be changed to meet experimental requirements.

56
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:59,379
Six detectors are used to obtain all pertinent information about the power level and its rate of change.

57
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:05,740
The power level of the core is adjusted by neutron-absorbing control rods.

58
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:12,780
Here is a core with four rods in position.

59
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:17,180
Three of these are safety rods containing boron carbide.

60
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:24,300
The fission rate and accompanying radiation increase as the stainless steel control rod is withdrawn.

61
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:28,720
The three safety rods are suspended by electromagnets.

62
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:36,860
For reactor shutdown, or scram, the rods are released and dropped into the core.

63
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:44,100
An unshielded core would, of course, emit intense radiation over a wide area.

64
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:53,080
The water around the core serves as a radiation shield.

65
00:07:53,080 --> 00:08:01,620
The reactor system now consists of a core in a large pool of water, control rods and

66
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:13,240
their drive mechanisms, a control room and detectors near the core to measure the level

67
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:23,379
neutron and gamma radiation. A view of the entire reactor area is available to

68
00:08:23,379 --> 00:08:29,400
the operator at the control console. An array of indicators is in front of the

69
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:38,139
console. Alarms and indicators on the console provide warning of trouble. They

70
00:08:38,139 --> 00:08:43,879
They indicate both the nature and location of the malfunction.

71
00:08:43,879 --> 00:08:48,019
Each warning alarm must be acknowledged.

72
00:08:48,019 --> 00:09:03,000
Here a chart drive was not turned on.

73
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:08,299
After the safety check is completed, the reactor is started up.

74
00:09:08,299 --> 00:09:13,860
The operator actuates a switch to withdraw the safety rods.

75
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:25,039
Their positions are indicated on the console in centimeters.

76
00:09:25,039 --> 00:09:33,639
After careful monitoring, the control rod is withdrawn.

77
00:09:33,639 --> 00:09:41,279
As the reactor is started, the increasing count rate from a fission chamber is observed.

78
00:09:41,279 --> 00:09:45,860
The period channel indication is a measure of the rate of increase.

79
00:09:45,860 --> 00:09:51,000
Too fast a period results in an automatic reactor shutdown.

80
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:55,779
Too high a power level also results in a scram.

81
00:09:55,779 --> 00:10:03,080
The power level is displayed on a linear scale.

82
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:06,799
And also on a logarithmic scale.

83
00:10:06,799 --> 00:10:12,480
Twenty minutes are required to raise the power to 100 kilowatts.

84
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:18,779
At about 1 kilowatt, the characteristic blue glow of the Cherenkov radiation surrounding

85
00:10:18,779 --> 00:10:20,779
the core becomes visible.

86
00:10:48,779 --> 00:10:59,819
The operator can shut down the system manually at any time.

87
00:11:18,779 --> 00:11:25,159
As a safety measure, all persons entering the experimental floor must signal the control operator,

88
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:30,860
who opens access doors by means of electrically operated locks.

89
00:11:35,399 --> 00:11:42,059
The main experimental area of the reactor facility is used for neutron beam research.

90
00:11:43,179 --> 00:11:48,120
Seven beam ports, which penetrate the thick shielding at the front face of the pool,

91
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:52,600
allow direct beams of neutrons to be used for research.

92
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:59,580
At this beam port, a neutron diffraction experiment is in progress.

93
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:03,899
A single crystal spectrometer is being used

94
00:12:03,899 --> 00:12:07,080
to study the magnetic structure of nickel oxide.

95
00:12:09,320 --> 00:12:13,299
At the adjoining port, a similar diffraction experiment

96
00:12:13,299 --> 00:12:17,600
is in progress on powder samples such as iron oxide.

97
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:23,899
The investigator is using magnetic scattering of neutrons to locate the

98
00:12:23,899 --> 00:12:30,560
lattice vacancies in the material. The neutron beam is directed by a collimator

99
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:38,139
onto the sample mounted on a goniometer. The sample can be rotated either

100
00:12:38,139 --> 00:12:44,919
manually or by a servo motor. The large horizontal cylinder is a borated

101
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:53,060
paraffin shield surrounding a boron trifluoride counter. A third diffraction

102
00:12:53,060 --> 00:13:00,420
apparatus is shown here. It is used to study the structure of single crystals.

103
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:08,200
The sample can be oriented with respect to the neutron beam. The detector arm can

104
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:14,300
be rotated through 175 degrees of arc.

105
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:20,759
Here a neutron crystal spectrometer uses Bragg reflections from a beryllium crystal to obtain

106
00:13:20,759 --> 00:13:23,660
mono-energetic neutrons.

107
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:28,300
It is being calibrated with known resonances of indium.

108
00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:35,180
The reactor beam port is out of sight behind stacks of paraffin shielding blocks.

109
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:41,700
Two indium foils are being rotated sequentially into the beam.

110
00:13:41,700 --> 00:13:47,080
The desired neutron energy is selected by automatically positioning the spectrometer

111
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:51,280
arm at an accurately known angle.

112
00:13:51,280 --> 00:13:57,300
The apparatus can be used for measurements of slow neutron resonances but is here being

113
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:03,460
employed as a source of monochromatic neutrons for the study of capture gamma ray spectra.

114
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:12,500
A different type of experiment is shown in this apparatus for investigating

115
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:17,540
the interaction of polarized neutrons with polarized nuclear samples.

116
00:14:19,060 --> 00:14:24,740
The neutrons are polarized and given the orientation desired by transmission through

117
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:31,620
magnetize iron. The nuclear samples are polarized by reducing the temperature to

118
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:36,799
near one one hundredth of a degree absolute and then applying a strong

119
00:14:36,799 --> 00:14:47,659
magnetic field. Here at the vertical axis to the thermal column, a physicist checks

120
00:14:47,659 --> 00:14:52,519
the thermal neutron response of an experimental proportional counter filled

121
00:14:52,519 --> 00:15:01,220
with boron trimethyl gas also on the balcony is the control center of the

122
00:15:01,220 --> 00:15:08,059
pneumatic rabbit system here material to be irradiated is being inserted into the

123
00:15:08,059 --> 00:15:17,059
rabbit system the system is used to transport samples to a point near the

124
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:22,440
reactor core where they are exposed for a predetermined time to radiation and

125
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:30,140
then sent to an outlet near a laboratory counting room. Here the rabbit is removed

126
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:38,080
from the pneumatic system, placed in a mechanical uncapping device,

127
00:15:47,060 --> 00:15:52,100
and the sample taken out.

128
00:16:04,420 --> 00:16:10,260
The activation of the sample is measured in a shielded, well-type scintillation counter.

129
00:16:17,060 --> 00:16:32,580
The scientist observes the decay of the activity as displayed by an automatic printout scaler.

130
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:38,060
Samples can also be irradiated in the pool.

131
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:44,060
Set into the rear wall of the pool are the hand-operated doors of water lock type transfer

132
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:47,060
leading to the adjoining hot cell facility.

133
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:58,060
The irradiated samples can be moved underwater into the transfer ports.

134
00:17:07,059 --> 00:17:12,059
The port door is then cranked closed and the water lock is drained.

135
00:17:15,059 --> 00:17:20,059
The hot cell is designed for killer-curie level activities.

136
00:17:24,059 --> 00:17:29,059
Adjacent to it is the radiation laboratory or manipulator room.

137
00:17:39,059 --> 00:17:43,059
From this room operators can look into the hot cell.

138
00:17:44,059 --> 00:17:53,399
Seen through the window, the inner gate of the water lock opens.

139
00:17:53,399 --> 00:17:59,740
The window consists of one-inch thick glass plates set three feet apart with an aqueous

140
00:17:59,740 --> 00:18:13,179
solution of zinc bromide in between.

141
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:17,840
The sample holder is withdrawn by a direct coupled manipulator.

142
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:23,840
A power-driven electromechanical manipulator is also available for handling heavy objects.

143
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:34,900
This, then, is the Naval Research Laboratory's latest addition to its family of investigative tools for nuclear experimentation.

144
00:19:34,900 --> 00:19:42,460
It was designed and built primarily for the conduct of basic research, and findings are

145
00:19:42,460 --> 00:19:46,660
made available to the public through the open literature of science.
